      <article id="squeeze" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1981 · CP/M &amp; MS-DOS</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Squeeze</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            One of the original compression formats used on the PC was known as File Squeezer, which was introduced in May 1981 for CP/M systems and later ported to the Microsoft Disk Operating System by various authors.
            CP/M was the first universal microcomputer operating system, existing before Microsoft's Windows, MS-DOS, and even the PC as a platform.
            On the PC, <a href="http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/Squeeze" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Squeezing</a> comprises two programs: <code>SQ.EXE</code>, the file squeezer for compression, and <code>USQ.EXE</code>, the file unsqueezer for decompression.
            Squeeze initially did not preserve file modification dates and could only compress individual files.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="lbr" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1982 · CP/M &amp; MS-DOS</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">LBR (Library Utility)</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The Library Utility is another CP/M tool, originally authored by Gary Novosielski in 1982
            and later ported to MS-DOS as <code>LU.EXE</code>.
            LBR files are simply uncompressed packages containing a collection of files, the microcomputer equivalent of an uncompressed TAR package.
            However, unlike today's TAR archives that preserve everything of the file system, LBR archives had major limitations:
            they could not preserve file dates, file permissions, or any sub-directories,
            as the whole concept of sub-directories and folders didn't exist on either CP/M or MS-DOS in 1982.
          </p>
          <p>
            Jim Kloss, author of NoChange BBS, <a href="/f/be268e1">writes</a>:
            <q>one of the best things to happen to us BBS'ers is the use of libraries to put many
            commonly related programs together for one simple download. This gets rid
            of the aggrevation you feel when you try to download all 9-10 files required
            to run some systems (i.e. RBBS). By putting them in a library, you can get them all in one pass.</q>
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="arc" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1985 · System Enhancement Associates</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">ARC (File Archive Utility)</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            The process of needing multiple unrelated utilities to archive, compress, and share packages online over bulletin boards was convoluted,
            and several solutions arrived on the PC to simplify the process.
            One of the first was ARC, "File Archive Utility", by Thom Henderson under the company System Enhancement Associates.
            This tool archived and compressed groups of files using a single command, and <a href="http://www.textfiles.com/programming/FORMATS/arc-lbr.pro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">caught on</a>
            in the PC BBS community, with many boards adopting it as a standard and repackaging their existing file offerings.
          </p>
          <p>
            The author of UNARC for CP/M systems <a href="https://www.cpc-power.com/index.php?page=detail&amp;onglet=notices&amp;num=13880" target="_blank" rel="noopener">notes</a> in May 1986:
            <q>The files which UNARC processes are the product of a utility program, ARC, which executes on 16-bit computers running the MS-DOS (or PC-DOS) operating system.
            This program has achieved widespread popularity since it was first introduced in March 1985.</q>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border rounded-3 bg-body-tertiary p-3 mt-4 index-prose">
          <h3 class="h6 text-uppercase text-body-secondary fw-semibold mb-2">Lost history</h3>
          <p class="small mb-0">
            Unfortunately, all this means there are no known Scene releases compressed with File Squeezer or packaged with the Library Utility.
            Everything preserved today has been repackaged using the ARC tool at a later date.
            For example, a release by the Duplicators for the game Bluebush Chess (<code>BBCHESS.COM</code>) was modified in September 1984,
            yet the package we have (<a href="/f/b627a2c">BLUEBUSH.ARC</a>) was created in June 1986.
            Another example is the SPI release of Shamus, which proclaims <q>&copy; Copyright invalid in 1984</q>, but our <a href="/f/b92e146">SHAMUS.ARC</a> package was made in December 1985.
            Both examples were probably repackaged by the group, a BBS, or a user to take advantage of the more convenient ARC tools.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="zoo" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1986 · Rahul Dhesi</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">Zoo (A Fast File Archiver)</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            A competing compression format known as <a href="http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/Zoo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zoo</a>, "A Fast File Archiver", by Rahul Dhesi, was released on the PC in August 1986.
            While it never caught on with the PC BBS Scene due to the popularity of ARC, Zoo proved more popular on the Commodore Amiga microcomputer, Unix, and early Linux.
            Unlike the earlier archiving tools, it also preserved file and folder directory structures.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="pkarc" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1985–1988 · Phil Katz</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">PKARC (The Faster ARC)</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            ARC had evolved into a standard on the PC by offering its source code for porting to other microcomputers and systems.
            While free, Thom, the author of ARC, asked for payments from users who thought the tools or the standard were useful.
          </p>
          <p>
            In late 1985, a programmer by the name of Phil Katz took a look at the source code and created a set of more optimized tools named PKARC
            and <a href="http://cd.textfiles.com/rbbsv3n1/pool/pkxarc.zip" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PKXARC</a>, "Fast! Archive Create/Update/Extract Utility", that handled ARC archives.
            He, too, <a href="http://cd.textfiles.com/rbbsv3n1/pool/pkx33a12.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">asked</a> for payments from users who found his faster tools useful, putting him in direct competition with Thom, the original author of ARC.
          </p>
          <p>
            Phil kept improving his tools and ultimately introduced new compression methods that broke compatibility with the ARC standard; however, he kept using the ARC branding and <code>.ARC</code> file extension.
            This confused users, forcing the adoption of Phil's tools over Thom's originals by following the later Microsoft playbook of embrace, extend, and extinguish.
            SEA responded by suing Phil for trademark and software likeness violations, and the two sides ultimately settled.
          </p>
          <p>
            Paul Homchick, in October 1988, <a href="http://bbsdocumentary.com/library/CONTROVERSY/LAWSUITS/SEA/arc-hist.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wrote</a>:
            <q>Phil Katz continued to produced new versions of PKARC, finally, in the latter part of 1987, introducing "SQUASHING" which was similar to zoo's 13-bit LZW compression.
            Although PKARC was no longer "ARC-compatible" it retained the .ARC extension.
            This caused confusion for novice users and a lot of grief for BBS operators.
            This policy was regarding by many as "standard busting," and likely was a factor in the litigation that followed in the summer of 1988.</q>
          </p>
          <p>
            Unfortunately for Thom, the litigation initiated an online <a href="http://www.bbsdocumentary.com/library/CONTROVERSY/LAWSUITS/SEA/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flame war</a> and the cancellation of the SEA brand.
            Many ARC users and BBS system operators sided with Phil, who presented himself as an underdog in a David and Goliath battle,
            with his supporters ditching SEA's tools and eventually leading to the demise of the tiny company and the abandonment of the ARC standard.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article id="pkzip" class="changelog-entry p-4">
        <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1989–1992 · PKWARE</div>
        <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">PKPAK &amp; PKZIP</h2>
        <div class="index-prose">
          <p>
            In the settlement, Phil agreed to rename his PKARC tools to PKPAK and PKUNPAK, but later retired them after introducing a new set of programs that were free of any source code or trademark violations.
            These tools were released as PKZIP and PKUNZIP and became the standard archiver for online boards and later the World Wide Web.
          </p>
          <p>
            Much like Phil had done earlier with his PKARC toolset, newer releases of PKZIP introduced incompatible compression methods while keeping the <code>.ZIP</code> file extension.
            This again led to confusion, with users not always knowing which archiving tool version to use for a particular ZIP archive.
          </p>
          <p>
            By the end of 1992, the ZIP standard eventually <a href="https://www.hanshq.net/zip.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">settled</a> on the "deflate" compression method first introduced in 1990, which is still used today.
            However, some of the more esoteric compression techniques found in ZIP files created before 1993 are often not usable with modern archiving tools or operating systems.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure d-block text-center mt-4 mb-0">
          <img src="/image/new/zip-error.webp" loading="lazy" class="img-fluid rounded border shadow-sm" alt="Error dialog extracting CONQUEST.ZIP">
          <figcaption class="figure-caption mt-2">Modern OS extraction failure on a pre-1993 ZIP archive.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <div id="rar-ace" class="row g-4" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;">
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1994 · Eugene Roshal</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">RAR (Roshal Archive)</h2>
            <p class="small mb-0">
              The <a href="http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/RAR" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roshal Archive</a> is a proprietary format developed by a sole engineer in 1994.
              RAR compressed packages are commonly used with large files on distributed networks such as BitTorrent where hosting or transfers may not always be stable,
              because RAR supports redundancy data that allows recovering individual files even when the package itself is incomplete.
              RAR decompression software is free, but creating archives requires a commercial product.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
          <article class="changelog-entry p-4 h-100">
            <div class="index-chapter-era font-monospace mb-1">1997 · Marcel Lemke</div>
            <h2 class="h4 fw-semibold mb-3">ACE</h2>
            <p class="small mb-0">
              ACE is another <a href="http://fileformats.archiveteam.org/wiki/ACE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">proprietary</a> format developed by a sole engineer and first released in 1997.
              It offered slightly better compression than RAR and briefly became popular when home Internet speeds were measured in kbit/s.
              Like RAR, ACE packages can be decompressed using free software, while compression required a commercial product.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

